<chapter xml:id="ch03-limits-and-continuity" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Limits and Continuity</title>

  <introduction>
    <p>Chapter 2 ended with a small but important calculation. For <m>s(t)=t^2</m>, the average velocity from <m>t=2</m> to <m>t=2+h</m> is</p>

    <md>\frac{s(2+h)-s(2)}{h}=4+h, \qquad h\neq 0.</md>

    <p>As <m>h</m> gets close to <m>0</m>, this average velocity gets close to <m>4</m>. The value <m>h=0</m> is forbidden in the quotient, but we can move as close to it as we like.</p>

    <p>That is the first shape of a limit: <em>the input approaches a value, and the output approaches a value.</em></p>

    <p>The word <q>approaches</q> sounds informal. This chapter turns it into mathematics.</p>
  </introduction>

